# S3 — who answers for the parts of the repository that, if wrong, take the system or its data down. Sensitive paths are listed first for the
# reader; the LAST matching line wins, so the general line is first and the sensitive ones after it.
#
# What this does and does not do: GitHub asks the owner for a review of a pull request that touches these paths. It is NOT a gate — the owner
# is also the author of the pull requests the assistant opens (it uses the owner's token) and cannot approve their own, so «require review from
# code owners» is deliberately off; the gate is the required CI checks (infra/github/protect-main.sh) and, for what the assistant changes, the risk
# label + the soak before a release (bridge/apps/backend/src/improve/pr-risk.ts, infra/autodeploy). The paths below are the ones pr-risk.ts calls
# `risk:high`; keep the two lists in step.
